From 1b4507dde5b8f74838b094d4998aa6cdce389d1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Avery Felts <averyfelts@aol.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:40:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 195/195] kernelbase: Counter-Strike 2 runs its job pool on 4
 threads.

cs2.exe in Counter-Strike Global Offensive\game\bin\win64 gets -threads 4,
unless its command line already has -threads.

With more threads (its default on a 12-core Mac, or 6 or 8), its D3D11
renderer crashed 25 to 45 s in on Mullion. The crash was in
rendersystemdx11's pool of recycled buffers: a lock-free queue whose nodes
come from tier0's CTSListBase had a node in the queue and in the free list
at once. A job thread (GlobPool) then read the null next of the queue's
head, at rendersystemdx11.dll+0x4a74e.

This is a race in the game's own lock-free code, and x86's memory ordering
doesn't prevent it either. None of these changed it:
- FEX's ordering emulation in full (vector and memcpy loads and stores too);
- strict split-lock emulation;
- multiblock off.

Only fewer threads did: 4 ran without a crash several times. Emulation
widens the window between the queue's reads and its compare-exchanges, and
Mullion's maps (about 25,000 WRITE_DISCARD maps a second) keep the pool busy.
DXVK's D3D11 and CS2's Vulkan renderer did not crash.

Steam's client starts CS2 from its 32-bit process, so the rule is in both
kernelbase builds.
---
 dlls/kernelbase/launchers.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/kernelbase/launchers.c b/dlls/kernelbase/launchers.c
index 39dededc126..c1dc0425530 100644
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/launchers.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/launchers.c
@@ -96,6 +96,23 @@ static const struct launcher_process_policy launcher_process_policies[] =
         {L"--type=", BATTLENET_MARKER, NULL},
         BATTLENET_ARGS,
     },
+    /* MetalSharp: Counter-Strike 2 runs its job pool on 4 threads (-threads 4), unless its command line names a count.
+     * With more (its default on a 12-core Mac, or 6 or 8), its D3D11 renderer crashed 25 to 45 s in on Mullion:
+     * rendersystemdx11's pool of recycled buffers, a lock-free queue with nodes from tier0's CTSListBase, had a node
+     * in the queue and in the free list at once, and a job thread (GlobPool) read the null next of the queue's head.
+     * It is a race in the game's own lock-free code, which x86's memory ordering doesn't prevent either: FEX's
+     * ordering emulation in full (vector and memcpy stores too), strict split locks, and multiblock off changed
+     * nothing; only fewer threads did. Emulation widens the window between the queue's reads and its compare-exchanges
+     * enough for 6 threads to hit it, and Mullion's rate of maps (25,000 a second) keeps the pool busy. */
+    {
+        L"cs2.exe",
+        L"\\Counter-Strike Global Offensive\\game\\bin\\win64\\",
+        L"/Counter-Strike Global Offensive/game/bin/win64/",
+        NULL,
+        NULL,
+        {L"-threads", NULL, NULL},
+        L" -threads 4",
+    },
     /* WineForge-Internal: launcher-compat/ubisoft-connect-in-process-gpu-v1. Its browser is upc.exe itself; its
      * renderers (UplayWebCore.exe --type=...) are left as they are. */
     {
